# ADR-2607161922: kotoba-lang/dtn gains static multi-hop relay routing (route-decision :relay) and fixes a destination-mismatch inbox bug

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-16
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki

## Problem

After ADR-2607161852 closed the durability and integrity gaps, `kotoba.dtn.router/route-decision`
still had only two outcomes: forward if the destination is a *directly*
reachable neighbor, or store. There was no way for a message to travel
through an intermediate node when no direct link to the final destination
existed — which is a real limitation on what a delay-tolerant *network* is
for (vs. point-to-point messaging with retry), and the next-highest-value
item from the gap inventory given when asked *「残りのgapは?」*.

Auditing the existing transport for this change surfaced a related latent
correctness bug: `kotoba.dtn.transport.tcp`'s inbound handler accepted any
successfully-decoded (and, if configured, signature-verified) bundle
straight into its own `:inbox`, without checking whether the bundle's
`:dtn/destination` actually matched the receiving node's own EID. That was
harmless while every message happened to be sent directly to its intended
recipient (the only pattern possible before this ADR), but would have been
silently wrong the moment relaying existed: a relay node would have
absorbed messages addressed to someone else as if they were its own.

## Decision

### Decision 1: `route-decision` gains an optional `:relay` action, not a rewrite of the existing two

`kotoba.dtn.router/route-decision`'s existing signature, direct-neighbor
behavior, and store-fallback behavior are all unchanged — every prior
caller and test keeps passing unmodified. New optional kwargs `:routes`
(a coll of `{:dtn/destination eid :dtn/next-hop eid}` entries — a
caller-supplied, statically-configured routing table), `:hop-count`
(default 0), and `:max-hops` (default 8) enable a third outcome: when no
direct link to the destination is reachable, but a `:routes` entry names a
next-hop that IS reachable and `:hop-count < :max-hops`, return
`{:dtn/action :relay :dtn/via <link> :dtn/next-hop <eid>}` — a result kept
structurally distinct from `:forward` (same wire action, different meaning:
\"this bundle isn't for whoever I hand it to next, it's a hop toward
someone else\"). Missing route, unreachable next-hop, or hop-count at/over
the cap all fall through to `:store`, exactly as the no-relay path already
did — a bundle that has hopped too many times without arriving gets held,
not endlessly bounced. This is deliberately **static/pre-configured
single-hop relay**, not automatic route discovery, topology advertisement,
or full Contact Graph Routing (RFC 9174) — one step beyond the prior
\"direct-neighbor-or-store\" heuristic, named as exactly that in the
namespace docstring, not oversold as general multi-hop routing.

### Decision 2: fix the destination-mismatch bug as part of this change, not separately

`kotoba.dtn.transport.tcp`'s inbound handler now checks
`(:dtn/destination bundle)` against the receiving node's own EID
immediately after (any configured) signature verification. A match keeps
the exact prior behavior (decode, add to `:inbox`, log `DTN-RECV`). A
mismatch is treated as a relay request: hop-count is incremented, a
distinct `DTN-RELAY` line is logged, and the bundle is re-submitted through
the same `route-and-send!` path a locally-originated send would use — using
the relay node's OWN `:links`/`:routes`, so it may forward further or store
if it, too, lacks a path. A relayed-through bundle is never added to the
relaying node's own `:inbox`. A lightweight per-node `:seen-bundle-ids` set
suppresses exact-duplicate re-relays of the same bundle id, a minimal,
honest loop-prevention addition alongside the hop-count cap — not a claim
of full duplicate-bundle detection across restarts or nodes.

### Decision 3: verify the relay is real, not merely a returned value

Demo scenario 6 configures node A with **no** peer/link entry for node C
at all — only for node B, plus a `:routes` entry naming C reachable via B —
and node B with a genuine direct link to C. A sends to C over real TCP.
Assertions check three things, not just \"did C receive it\": C's `:inbox`
actually contains the message; B's `:inbox` does **NOT** contain it
(proving the Decision 2 fix — B correctly relayed instead of absorbing);
and B's own log shows it made a real `DTN-RELAY` routing decision (not an
out-of-band shortcut in the test harness). All five prior scenarios (1
through 4b) were re-run unmodified and still pass.

As with the two prior follow-up ADRs, this was independently re-verified
by the orchestrating session from a cold clone before writing this ADR's
Verification section, not trusted from the implementing agent's report
alone.

## Verification

- `clojure -M:test` → 27 tests / 126 assertions (was 112; +14 new: 8 router relay/hop-count/max-hops cases plus supporting coverage), 0 failures, 0 errors. Independently re-run from a cold clone.
- `clojure -M:lint` → 0 errors, 0 warnings. Independently re-run.
- `nbb --classpath \"src:test:../phone/src:../html/src:../css/src\" test/kotoba/dtn/transport/tcp_demo.cljs` → `RESULT: 6/6 scenarios passed`, exit 0 (scenarios 1-4b from the prior two ADRs unmodified and still passing; new scenario 5/6 — A→B→C relay — passes, with B's own `DTN-RELAY` log line and B's untouched `:inbox` both confirmed). Independently re-run from a cold clone, output matches the implementing agent's report.
- Pushed to `github.com/kotoba-lang/dtn` (public), commit `54fe04c34eaa5c92f561956ec7e64fd33ce89301`.
- `manifest/west.yml`: pin advanced via `--entry dtn` (1-line diff, `a89edf50cfcc→54fe04c34eaa`, `verify-west-pins: 1 件の pin 変更をすべて検証 OK` — 3 unrelated pending pin changes elsewhere in the tree were correctly left untouched by the `--only dtn` scoping), landed via an isolated sibling-path worktree + GitHub API server-side merge, matching all three prior ADRs' landing procedure.

## Consequences

- `kotoba-lang/dtn` can now deliver a message through one statically
  configured intermediate relay when no direct link to the final
  destination exists — a genuine step from \"point-to-point messaging with
  retry\" toward \"a network,\" which is what the whole design exists to be.
- The destination-mismatch fix (Decision 2) closes a latent correctness
  bug that existed since ADR-2607161817's original transport, even though
  it never manifested under that ADR's only-ever-direct-send usage pattern
  — worth recording plainly as a bug found and fixed while adding a
  feature, not just a new capability.
- Relay routing is single-hop only (A relays via B to reach C; A relaying
  via B via D to reach C — i.e. more than one intermediate hop — is not
  tested and not claimed, though `route-decision`'s recursive-feeling
  structure at the transport layer, where each relaying node re-runs its
  own `route-and-send!`, suggests it may already work for longer chains;
  this ADR only verifies and claims the two-hop case actually demonstrated
  in scenario 6).
- Still explicitly undone, unchanged from the prior two ADRs' inventories:
  automatic route discovery/advertisement (`kotoba-lang/net` gossip
  composition), NAT traversal (`kotoba-lang/turn` composition), PKI/replay
  protection, remaining RFC 9171 wire-format details, mesh-radio/satellite
  hardware, real carrier gateway credentials, a consuming cloud-itonami
  actor.

## Alternatives considered

- **Dynamic route discovery/advertisement** (nodes announce reachability to
  each other, building routing tables automatically). Rejected for this
  ADR: materially larger scope, edges into `kotoba-lang/net`'s gossip
  domain already named as deferred composition work, and risks reproducing
  the \"honestly-scoped heuristic vs. full CGR\" line this repo has drawn
  since ADR-2607161743 — static/configured routes keep the claim exactly
  matched to what was built and verified.
- **Per-node visited-node-list loop prevention** (each bundle carries the
  list of nodes it's already passed through) instead of a simple hop-count
  cap. Rejected as more machinery than this pass's honest scope needs; a
  hop-count cap plus a duplicate-bundle-id suppression set catches the
  practical failure modes (misconfigured route cycles, redundant re-relay)
  without adding a growing per-bundle field.
- **Deferring the destination-mismatch fix to a separate ADR/PR.**
  Rejected: the bug was inert before relay routing existed and only
  becomes a real hazard once relaying is added, so fixing it in the same
  change that introduces the hazard is the coherent unit of work — not a
  scope-creep addition.

